moral or political principle; traitorous to allegiance; false; renegade: as, “the ''apostate'' lords,” ''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., i.

¶
'''apostate†''' (ạ-pos′tāt), ''v. i.'' [< ''apostate'', ''n.''] To apostatize.
{{smaller block|Had Peter been truly inspired by God, . . . he would not have ''apostated'' from his purpose.
{{right|''Fuller.''}}}}

'''apostatical''' (ap-os-tat′i-kal), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀποστατικός, rebellious, < ἀποστάτης: see ''apostate''.] Apostate; guilty of or characterized by apostasy.
{{smaller block|An hereticall and ''apostaticall'' church.
{{right|''Bp. Hall.''}}}}
{{smaller block|An assembly of prelates, convened by Archbishop Usher in 1626, declared that the religion of Papists is superstitious and idolatrous; their faith and doctrines erroneous and heretical; their Church, in respect to both, ''apostatical.''
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., vi.}}}}

'''apostatize''' (ạ-pos′tạ̄-tīz), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''apostatized'', ppr. ''apostatizing''. [< ML. ''apostatizare'', < LL. ''apostata'', apostate: see ''apostate'' and ''-ize''.] To abandon one’s profession or church; forsake one’s principles; retrograde from one’s faith; withdraw from one’s party. Also spelled ''apostatise''.
{{smaller block|He ''apostatized'' from his old faith in facts, took to believing in semblances.
{{right|''Carlyle.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The English certainly were not converted to Christianity: did the Britons ''apostatize'' to heathendom?
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Amer. Lects., p. 131.}}}}

'''apostaxis''' (ap-os-tak′sis), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀπόσταξις, < ἀποστάζειν, drip, distil, < ἀπό, away, from, + στάζειν, drip.] '''1.''' In ''med.'', the defluxion of any fluid, as of blood from the nose.—'''2.''' In ''bot.'', an abnormal discharge of the juices of plants, as the gumming of the plum.

